WEBVTT

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:23.040
Hello and welcome to Thanet Earth in Kent, Britain's largest hi-tech farm.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:33.960
Under these glass roofs, an area the size of 49 football pitches on a farm without soil.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:42.160
They grow hundreds of thousands of tonnes of fruit and veg that we can eat all year round.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:44.520
Britain is a nation that loves its food.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.840
We grow it, buy it and eat it on an epic scale.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:54.920
The way in which we create and consume food is changing now faster than ever.

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:57.000
I'm ready to shop if you are.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:59.600
So what is just around the corner?

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:06.239
This series will change the way we think about the food we eat for ever.

00:01:05.319 --> 00:01:15.200
I'll be joined by a team of experts and we'll scour the globe to find you the amazing future of breakfast, lunch and dinner.

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:22.080
Tonight, Michelin-starred chef Angela Hartnett is leaving the safety of her kitchen...

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:22.400
Oh, crikey!

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:28.320
..And joining the US Army to bring us food that never goes off.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:32.960
Could this be the end of the sell-by date?

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:33.880
Hello, Mark.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:38.480
Fruit and veg expert Chris Bavin heads to Australia to visit the world's most robotic farm.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:44.080
Can bots help us grow cheaper and healthier food?

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:46.440
It's like a deadly weed sniper, isn't it?

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:54.400
Our technology expert Dr Shini Somara is in China to ask is this really what our future waiters will look like?

00:01:53.600 --> 00:02:00.240
A seemingly sci-fi future is actually a reality.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:06.200
And I'll be finding out how to protect our crops from extreme weather.

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:10.160
And this is how you make it rain, Texas style.

00:02:09.199 --> 00:02:15.480
This is Tomorrow's Food.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:27.280
To kick the series off, I'm off to see something that could revolutionise the future of our fast food.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:33.040
It could give us hot meals 24/7 and is right here in London.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:37.440
The world's fastest takeaway pizza.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:45.280
Well, that's not official, but it is freshly made pizza straight out of a machine.

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:52.840
This pizza vending machine can knock you up a pizza from scratch and it can do it in just three minutes.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:55.640
So let's give it a go.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:56.240
You fancy some pizza?

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:57.280
Yes.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:57.560
Of course.

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:59.240
I'm hungry.

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:03.880
The machine mixes fresh dough for every order...

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:07.080
You can actually see the pizza being made in there.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:08.800
Wow.

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:12.000
..Sprays out tomato sauce with a robotic arm...

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.200
Oh, look.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:17.640
That is sick.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:20.000
..Adds a topping in one specially made layer...

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:21.760
I think it needs more pepperoni, though.

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.800
Oh, does it?

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:28.280
Just send it back?

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:32.480
..And after cooking in less than a minute in an infrared oven...

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:34.560
Have you ever seen anything like this before?

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:35.400
No.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:36.560
..Out pops a piping hot pizza.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:37.040
Wow.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:41.480
Yeah, it's all right.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:43.880
Needs more cheese.

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:53.760
If they can do this with pizza, what's next?

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:58.840
Coin operated hamburgers or vending machine kebabs?

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:06.640
So far this is the only machine like this in the country, but soon you may well be seeing pizza vending machines popping up on a high street near you.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:10.920
It's actually very good.

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:30.000
Now I've come to Britain's largest and most futuristic greenhouse farm.

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:39.600
You say farm to people, they think of mud and fields and battling the British weather.

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:40.880
Not here.

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:49.080
Everything is controlled, the lights, the temperature, the insects, there isn't even any soil.

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:57.840
So is this what all our farms will soon look like?

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:14.080
These hi-tech greenhouses hold the secret to putting 16 million peppers, 13 million cucumbers and a whopping 430 million tomatoes onto our plates every year.

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:22.080
You're the man in charge of this huge operation.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:22.400
Give me a sense of the scale.

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:25.280
How many tomato plants do you have in here?

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:27.160
In total we have about 1.5 million plants here.

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:29.040
Wow.

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:34.560
The most obvious difference in somebody's garden at home, or how any of us grow tomatoes is, there's no soil.

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:37.400
No, we grow in rock wool.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:47.600
Instead of soil, the roots of the tomatoes are planted into a dense wool made from volcanic rock.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:49.760
So the roots are right in here.

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:51.600
The roots go straight through it?

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:51.920
Yes.

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:53.160
So it's like a...

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:55.200
Oh, it just feels like loft insulation.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:01.000
So you plant them in this artificial looking substance, not soil, what's the problem with soil?

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:03.120
Growing in soil gives us less control.

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:04.120
This is all about control.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:07.440
The more control we have, the better the crops we can grow.

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:15.640
The rock wool holds more water than soil and it doesn't have any of the dangerous bacteria that soil can contain.

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:22.320
So, counterintuitively, tomato plants grow better out of mud and earth than they do...?

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:23.640
Absolutely.

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:33.160
Soil or not, tomatoes need food, and here tiny pipes feed in precise levels of nutrients and water.

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:37.320
All controlled by computer.

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:42.520
But the thing that really produces a bumper crop is putting the fear of death into them.

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:53.800
By quickly dropping the temperature at the end of the day that tricks the plant to thinking it's going to die and it wants to reproduce, to preserve the species, so it will get forced into fruit production.

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:54.160
Cos that happens in nature.

00:06:53.240 --> 00:07:04.320
In the autumn, the temperature quickly drops, which is naturally their end of life, so they start focusing on fruit production.

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:13.320
This might seem like a dirty trick, but by fooling the plants into thinking they're dying they trigger their survival mechanism.

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:19.080
And survival depends on reproduction and that means producing more tomatoes.

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:23.320
How much more efficient is this at producing tomatoes than just traditional farming methods?

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:23.960
I would say four, 500%.

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:25.000
Wow.

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:30.520
Four, five times more yield because of the way you do it?

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:32.200
Correct.

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:33.840
That's incredible.

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:35.280
So all of us with our grow bags at home, plonking things in...

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:37.520
Forget it.

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:38.320
..We've been doing it wrong for years.

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:48.400
We'll be back here later to see just what Gert and his team do when their crops come under attack.

00:07:48.360 --> 00:08:00.400
But their top tip, by the way, if you want to improve your home-grown tomatoes is to keep the plant thirsty, particularly when it's young, and you'll end up with tastier fruit.

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:18.440
Whether it's a cream bun or a traditional fry-up, we all know that too much fat is bad for our waistlines.

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:26.560
But there may be a secret ingredient that could let us eat fat and not pile on the pounds.

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:33.679
Of course it sounds too good to be true, so we sent Chris Bavin to found out more.

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:47.720
I'm here on the Northumberland coast, but I'm not here for future ice creams or fish and chips from a far-flung land, I'm here for something a little less tasty.

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:50.600
Brown seaweed.

00:08:50.800 --> 00:09:01.960
It contains a harmless chemical called alginate that scientists believe can stop our bodies absorbing some of the fat we eat.

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:07.960
So we're going to put this to the test on a group with a reputation for being heavier than most.

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:12.960
Truckers.

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:18.120
Hello, hello.

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:19.240
Good morning.

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:24.160
You could have waited for me, you've already started tucking in, I see.

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:28.520
So when you're out on the road, what sort of foods are you eating on a daily basis?

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:31.120
Fried breakfast.

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:33.840
Steak pie and chips for dinner.

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:36.080
Chinese takeaways.

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:36.560
This is just the starter.

00:09:36.680 --> 00:09:38.360
By the time you finish that you'll be ready for lunch, eh?

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:40.960
That's a lot of high-fat food.

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:45.960
But can seaweed help the truckers to lose weight without changing the way they eat?

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:48.800
We aren't going to ask them to eat seaweed.

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:58.680
To be effective, the alginate needs to concentrated into pills which have to be taken with every meal.

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:07.960
And to see if they work we're going to weigh and measure them now and again in four weeks' time.

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:08.800
So for that, I'm going to need you guys to take your shoes and socks off, please.

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:09.640
Is that all right?

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:13.440
You can keep your pants on, don't worry.

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:21.360
Dr Matt Wilcox, the man who helped make the link between seaweed and fat, is running the trial.

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:29.000
He's also giving them a blood test and is working out just what percentage of their body is fat.

00:10:29.720 --> 00:10:30.920
25% body fat.

00:10:30.040 --> 00:10:32.360
Nah, your machine doesn't work properly.

00:10:31.480 --> 00:10:35.160
Recalibrate it.

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:37.520
Your fat percentage, 41.9.

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:43.480
Fit as a butcher's dog.

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:43.800
I thought I'd better give it a go as well.

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:48.680
They asked me to fatten up for this, actually.

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:51.200
I'm a method actor, so...

00:10:51.920 --> 00:11:00.200
The healthy range depends on your age, but for blokes like me it's between eight and 20%.

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:02.920
And if you're a bit older it's 11-25.

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:06.840
25.3% fat.

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:09.000
It's quite a lot, isn't it?

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:22.160
So how is the seaweed alginate going to help the truckers and my vital stats move in the right direction?

00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:25.320
It's all to do with how the fat we eat gets processed by our bodies.

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:36.520
Usually fat is broken down in the gut and then absorbed into the blood where it can travel around the body to be stored or processed.

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:40.640
So if we take, for example, we're in a cafe now, so if we take a full English breakfast, OK?

00:11:39.800 --> 00:11:42.480
If you normally eat that, how much of that will your body absorb and digest?

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:51.680
So your body's really efficient in digesting fat, so 95 to 100% of all the fat on that plate will be absorbed.

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:52.320
Wow.

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:58.560
And then it moves from the blood to various parts of the body and it's stored, it's fat, so whether that's your stomach or your hips.

00:11:57.880 --> 00:12:08.280
So if you can reduce the amount that's digested and absorbed then you can reduce the amount that goes into your blood and therefore reduce the amount that's stored.

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:17.920
And this is what it's hoped seaweed alginate can do as it stops some of the fat passing from the gut into the bloodstream.

00:12:17.080 --> 00:12:21.880
So where does it go if it doesn't go into your blood, what happens?

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:22.200
It just passes out naturally, so it will come out in your stool.

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:24.240
Wow.

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:27.760
So will the seaweed work for our truckers and me?

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:32.040
We'll be back later to find out.

00:12:41.120 --> 00:12:46.360
Next, what will tomorrow's restaurants look like?

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:49.080
Eating out in Britain is a big deal.

00:12:48.200 --> 00:13:02.560
There's over 100,000 places to get a meal, from fine dining to fast food, from pubs to takeaways, and in that competitive clamour, places are always trying to find new, inventive ways to feed us.

00:13:01.720 --> 00:13:12.920
To see what's going to come next, technology expert Shini Somara travelled to China to visit two restaurants that are unlike anywhere you've ever eaten before.

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:28.040
I grew up with Meccano sets and mechanical gadgets, so a restaurant I would like to try is one run by machines.

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:35.680
And in this place, just outside Shanghai, that seemingly sci-fi future is actually a reality.

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:43.080
In this restaurant, robots run the show, waiting tables and working as chefs.

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:51.000
The waiters glide around using optical sensors that follow a clear black line on the floor.

00:13:51.080 --> 00:13:56.840
They deliver your food, are programmed to say nice things... ..and there is even a little bit of flirting!

00:13:55.960 --> 00:14:01.280
And when you want them to leave, just tap them on the head.

00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:03.800
Back in the kitchen, there is no danger of getting a hair in your soup.

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:11.440
These robots steam hundreds of dumplings a day, but they can't do everything.

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:19.040
So, it still needs a human being to actually serve my dumplings on a plate, which is kind of reassuring, because for a robot to cook my entire meal, I'm not sure I'm too comfortable with!

00:14:26.200 --> 00:14:33.480
In theory, it seems like an incredibly efficient way to run a restaurant.

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:40.040
But now, having seen them in the flesh, they feel like more of a novelty - essentially trays on wheels.

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:45.920
Look, I think they are fun, I just don't think they are the future.

00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:53.360
But on the other side of Shanghai, I've heard of a very different restaurant that has recently opened.

00:14:52.480 --> 00:14:55.760
One that is so extraordinary that its location is kept secret.

00:14:57.200 --> 00:15:13.680
I'm sitting in a specially built dining room that is using new science to control the way my food tastes by stimulating all of my senses.

00:15:12.800 --> 00:15:19.440
This is such a weird sensation, it feels like I'm sinking below the earth!

00:15:20.120 --> 00:15:25.120
Recent discoveries have revealed that it's not just our taste buds that allow us to enjoy our meals.

00:15:24.200 --> 00:15:29.840
And here, they are putting that into practice.

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:40.400
By manipulating the sights, sounds and smells around me, they can change the way my food tastes.

00:15:40.200 --> 00:15:44.640
There was a big gust of cigar smoke underneath that glass dome.

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:48.680
My senses are kind of overwhelmed!

00:15:48.040 --> 00:15:55.840
They've created a different scene for every course.

00:15:55.320 --> 00:15:58.280
I'm about to eat an oyster.

00:15:57.600 --> 00:16:06.200
So, I can really smell the ocean and the air, and then, seeing water on the walls really makes me feel like I've just picked this out of the ocean.

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:21.240
The restaurant only seats ten diners, but requires a team of 25 to produce a 22-course meal.

00:16:20.440 --> 00:16:28.680
The whole experience is the brainchild of French chef Paul Pairet.

00:16:27.880 --> 00:16:29.480
So, what was your vision in creating all of this?

00:16:28.640 --> 00:16:37.560
Compared to normal restaurants, the possibility was to trigger the atmosphere, so how do you trigger the atmosphere?

00:16:36.720 --> 00:16:38.640
Basically, you've got to play on everything you can play on.

00:16:37.760 --> 00:16:47.040
We can play on the smell, we play on the sound, on the music, you can play on the visual.

00:16:46.080 --> 00:16:53.400
Delivering Chef Paul's vision requires some cutting-edge technology.

00:16:55.720 --> 00:17:09.520
To run the dining room takes eight miles of cabling, seven hi-res video projectors, four scent machines pumping out tailor-made perfumes, and dozens of lights and speakers.

00:17:08.560 --> 00:17:15.480
All managed by ten computers from a specially designed control room.

00:17:14.520 --> 00:17:24.359
This beautiful piece of fish that's being revealed, the guests look really stunned, I don't think they know what they are in for next!

00:17:23.599 --> 00:17:27.720
It's almost like watching a performance.

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:31.200
If this is what the future is like, then I'm really excited.

00:17:30.280 --> 00:17:34.200
Although it does come with a huge price tag.

00:17:33.320 --> 00:17:35.800
Each meal costs a staggering £400.

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:45.320
But using our senses to change the way we taste isn't just for fancy restaurants.

00:17:44.360 --> 00:17:55.960
If you are on a night out and don't want to drink too much, then avoid pubs with loud music - the noise is thought to cause stress, making you drink more.

00:17:55.000 --> 00:18:13.480
And if you are eating on a plane, be aware that the food will have more seasoning - scientific studies have shown it would taste bland without it.

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:21.320
That's because our brains are so busy blocking out the noise from the engines.

00:18:23.680 --> 00:18:29.120
Now, can you make cheap wine taste like posh plonk?

00:18:28.280 --> 00:18:32.480
Two deep-fried oysters, one baked pumpkin.

00:18:31.720 --> 00:18:38.440
Throughout the series, our very own Michelin-starred chef Angela Hartnett...

00:18:38.760 --> 00:18:44.160
..Is going to be trying out some of the kitchen gadgets...

00:18:43.200 --> 00:18:49.400
..That claim they could revolutionise our cooking and our lives.

00:18:48.520 --> 00:18:51.960
Today, a technology that could transform wine.

00:18:51.120 --> 00:19:04.040
So, this is a wine decanter, and it claims that by using sonic waves, it can make every bottle of wine taste better.

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:04.800
Sounds too good to be true for me, but we're going to try it out.

00:19:04.080 --> 00:19:05.960
'To put it to the test...'

00:19:05.080 --> 00:19:06.200
In it goes.

00:19:05.600 --> 00:19:12.200
'I've got a £5 bottle of classic supermarket plonk.'

00:19:11.320 --> 00:19:18.200
You press red for a red wine... and we wait and see what happens.

00:19:17.240 --> 00:19:27.080
'The inventors claim that by passing high-frequency sound waves 'through the wine, their machine can mimic the ageing process.'

00:19:26.480 --> 00:19:35.880
Usually over time, chemicals in wine break down, smoothing and enhancing the flavour.

00:19:35.080 --> 00:19:37.600
But this can take years.

00:19:38.640 --> 00:19:46.360
The sonic decanter claims to do the same thing using sound waves... ..in just minutes.

00:19:45.520 --> 00:19:47.960
But does it work?

00:19:47.320 --> 00:19:51.280
'I'm going to compare one glass that has been through the decanter 'with one that hasn't.'

00:19:52.720 --> 00:19:57.200
So, they look the same colour-wise at the moment, they don't seem visibly any different.

00:19:58.240 --> 00:20:02.760
I can't smell any difference to them.

00:20:01.920 --> 00:20:04.640
So, let's taste it and see.

00:20:16.280 --> 00:20:21.760
Actually, that is a lot smoother, that is quite a noticeable difference.

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:26.600
I have to say, I thought that machine was just a load of old nonsense when I first saw it!

00:20:26.080 --> 00:20:31.360
I'm very surprised, I never thought that was going to work.

00:20:31.320 --> 00:20:34.560
Darn it!

00:20:41.520 --> 00:20:43.440
Don't just take my word for it.

00:20:43.040 --> 00:20:48.680
We took the decanter out onto the streets to give it a proper airing.

00:20:49.480 --> 00:20:52.080
Do I need to drink it all?

00:20:51.200 --> 00:21:00.720
Sample A came straight out of the bottle, while sample B has been treated with the sonic decanter.

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:02.000
The question is...

00:21:01.160 --> 00:21:04.760
Oh, yeah, you can definitely notice the taste difference.

00:21:03.800 --> 00:21:05.120
Mm.

00:21:04.160 --> 00:21:06.880
..Which tastes better?

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:11.960
I prefer this one here, B, less sharp, slightly easier on the palate.

00:21:11.280 --> 00:21:12.480
A.

00:21:11.600 --> 00:21:14.160
I prefer A.

00:21:13.280 --> 00:21:15.520
Prefer B.

00:21:14.600 --> 00:21:17.680
They both taste exactly the same.

00:21:16.720 --> 00:21:19.720
I like B, it's more fruity.

00:21:18.840 --> 00:21:20.760
B, B is the best.

00:21:22.280 --> 00:21:25.680
So, that's a pretty even split, then.

00:21:24.760 --> 00:21:31.680
Watch out for a sonic decanter coming your way soon!

00:21:40.760 --> 00:21:47.640
Earlier, here at Thanet Earth, we got a glimpse of future farming.

00:21:47.040 --> 00:21:50.400
But what about the farmers themselves?

00:21:49.800 --> 00:21:53.000
How might they change in tomorrow's world?

00:21:52.680 --> 00:21:59.440
To find out, we sent our fruit and veg expert Chris Bavin to Australia.

00:22:02.360 --> 00:22:10.480
I've come to a farm just outside Sydney where they are testing a new breed of farmer, a robot farmer!

00:22:13.440 --> 00:22:19.600
On this farm, I'm surrounded by a gang of futuristic machines, scouring the fields...

00:22:18.640 --> 00:22:24.640
..and patrolling the skies.

00:22:23.920 --> 00:22:29.840
These robots are one-of-a-kind prototypes, built by the University of Sydney.

00:22:28.960 --> 00:22:34.080
They've been designed to make farming quicker and cheaper.

00:22:33.280 --> 00:22:40.280
The first is a solar-powered weed hunter called Ladybird.

00:22:40.080 --> 00:22:50.800
The brains behind this future farmer are Mark Calleija and James Underwood.

00:22:49.840 --> 00:22:51.320
So, this is the Ladybird.

00:22:50.360 --> 00:22:52.720
What's it doing?

00:22:51.760 --> 00:22:55.840
Right now, it's looking for weeds, basically.

00:22:55.160 --> 00:23:04.960
The Ladybird hunts the fields alone, powered by solar panels and directed using an armoury of sensors that allow it to navigate the crops.

00:23:14.400 --> 00:23:21.520
When it spots one of its weedy victims, its high precision sprayer takes aim.

00:23:22.560 --> 00:23:27.640
The secret to how the Ladybird works is under its wings.

00:23:27.000 --> 00:23:39.000
So, there's a whole lot of different sensors around, there's a couple of cameras under here, looking straight down, and just like the way you use two eyes to see in 3D, those cameras can get a three-dimensional colour picture of what's going on under here.

00:23:38.120 --> 00:23:42.560
We can identify the targets, the weeds, send the arm to the right position to nail them with the herbicide.

00:23:47.760 --> 00:23:56.520
Then, an onboard computer takes aim and directs the weedkiller to just the right spots.

00:23:55.560 --> 00:23:56.840
It's like a deadly weed sniper, isn't it?

00:23:58.560 --> 00:24:05.240
And this is only the prototype version, it's going to get a lot faster and even more accurate.

00:24:05.400 --> 00:24:14.760
I tell you what, all you've got to teach it to do now is pick the crops and put it in a bag, and you guys will be billionaires!

00:24:14.080 --> 00:24:16.400
That's amazing, this is incredible!

00:24:15.560 --> 00:24:20.440
The Ladybird uses a fraction of the chemicals used in traditional crop spraying.

00:24:19.560 --> 00:24:28.720
And you never know, they might even build a mini Ladybird to weed our own back gardens.

00:24:27.800 --> 00:24:34.200
But the future of farming isn't just at ground level.

00:24:36.640 --> 00:24:46.520
Meet the farming drone, a flying shepherd that can find your missing cattle or even tell you when your crops are ready.

00:24:46.640 --> 00:24:49.240
In a similar way to how we use the cameras on the Ladybird, we can do that from a drone from above as well.

00:24:48.480 --> 00:24:58.160
So ultimately, you could have a farmer sat at home, on his computer, assessing vast scales of land, without even leaving his house?

00:24:57.280 --> 00:24:58.480
That's exactly right.

00:24:57.520 --> 00:25:07.640
And also, that farmer doesn't even have to actually fly that vehicle himself, the objective can be specified, like, "Go out, find my cows," like that sort of level, and then the drone can just take it from there.

00:25:13.240 --> 00:25:19.000
I've got one final bot to meet.

00:25:18.280 --> 00:25:28.040
A robot that could replace one of the most cherished guardians of a farm - the noble sheepdog.

00:25:28.160 --> 00:25:29.520
The sheepdog of the future might look something more like this.

00:25:29.440 --> 00:25:33.920
It's called the Shrimp and it's a robot herder.

00:25:34.280 --> 00:25:39.560
Though at the moment, it's only for cows.

00:25:40.440 --> 00:25:46.760
We've got this onboard laser sensor that scans around and we can see exactly where every single cow is.

00:25:45.800 --> 00:25:47.280
Is this what Shrimp is looking at right now?

00:25:46.360 --> 00:25:49.760
Yeah, exactly, it's kind of like seeing the world through Shrimp's eyes, if you like.

00:25:51.880 --> 00:26:02.080
Today, the Shrimp still needs a handler, and there is a lot to do before it can operate alone.

00:26:01.920 --> 00:26:06.840
So, of all the bots I've seen, my money's on the Ladybird.

00:26:06.080 --> 00:26:08.920
That feels like something that's going to make it really big!

00:26:10.160 --> 00:26:17.760
This has been an amazing look at a multi-million pound robot farm.

00:26:17.000 --> 00:26:19.480
We are in the future.

00:26:18.640 --> 00:26:30.680
These farm bots can help farmers not only save huge amount of time, but money as well, and put an end to some of the most backbreaking jobs there are.

00:26:41.800 --> 00:26:51.920
Back at the UK's largest and most advanced greenhouse, they don't rely on tech for everything.

00:26:51.120 --> 00:26:56.760
Sometimes, nature has a better way.

00:26:55.840 --> 00:27:02.920
Like when it comes to protecting these 1.5 million plants from billions of insects.

00:27:01.960 --> 00:27:07.320
'The resident bug expert is Dr Joe Burman.'

00:27:06.600 --> 00:27:11.440
Joe, thank you, that's quite an entrance we've made!

00:27:10.560 --> 00:27:12.920
Not bad, eh?

00:27:13.040 --> 00:27:18.160
Oh, you get a really good view of just how huge this is!

00:27:17.240 --> 00:27:18.480
Yeah.

00:27:17.520 --> 00:27:25.080
'With so many plants under one roof, 'an insect invasion would be a disaster.'

00:27:24.240 --> 00:27:27.200
Things like aphids and whitefly, they are a big problem.

00:27:26.360 --> 00:27:33.200
Like, an aphid arriving here, a single aphid, how much could that breed over a short period of time?

00:27:33.280 --> 00:27:42.240
Aphids are amazing things, so, for most of their lives, they are asexual, asexual organisms, just producing clones of themselves all the time.

00:27:41.480 --> 00:27:44.720
Yeah.

00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:48.640
Those clonal copies produce really quickly, so they can give birth, say, every 40 minutes, 45 minutes, something like that.

00:27:47.720 --> 00:27:49.000
That fast!

00:27:49.080 --> 00:27:57.840
'That means in just one season, one aphid could 'turn into 100 billion aphids, which would decimate these crops.'

00:27:56.880 --> 00:28:00.600
It's not pouring pesticides onto the plants?

00:27:59.760 --> 00:28:07.600
No, so that's the common misconception about producing plants in the UK, especially tomatoes.

00:28:06.920 --> 00:28:10.560
Most tomatoes and most glasshouse produce in the UK is pesticide-free, most of the time.

00:28:09.760 --> 00:28:13.080
So, what kills those aphids?

00:28:12.360 --> 00:28:30.320
So, we can actually have someone farm and produce lots and lots of predators, things which like to eat the aphids, so we have a species called Macrolophus, which is actually a related species to the aphids, and it has kind of a big, spiky mouth part, a little bit like a straw.

00:28:29.360 --> 00:28:30.520
Yeah.

00:28:29.720 --> 00:28:30.840
It can insert that into the aphid and it'll suck all the goodness out.

00:28:29.960 --> 00:28:34.520
So, you bring in another insect to eat the insects that eat the plants?

00:28:34.600 --> 00:28:38.240
That's right.

00:28:38.720 --> 00:28:43.960
So, they can quash an aphid invasion with one invited guest.

00:28:43.080 --> 00:28:46.000
But there is another they need even more.

00:28:46.080 --> 00:28:58.840
The most essential thing we need is pollinators, so, tomatoes have flowers, of course, the flowers need to be pollinated in order to produce the fruit, so we use bumblebees here.

00:28:58.000 --> 00:29:10.120
Bumblebees have got a particular kind of pollination, called buzz pollination, so they produce a particular frequency and they buzz in a particular way that vibrates the pollen and spreads it out and allows that flower to be pollinated.

00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:15.360
Would this building be economically viable, would it deliver enough tomatoes if it were not for bees?

00:29:14.400 --> 00:29:17.080
No, not a chance, no.

00:29:16.320 --> 00:29:19.080
Not at all.

00:29:18.280 --> 00:29:24.720
It produces a much bigger fruit, you get much more yield and the quality of the fruit is better.

00:29:23.960 --> 00:29:25.240
So, the bees are a vital part of the production process?

00:29:24.360 --> 00:29:25.800
Absolutely essential.

00:29:34.160 --> 00:29:39.080
Now, could the future bring us everlasting food?

00:29:38.160 --> 00:29:45.680
Angela's in Boston, on exercise with the US military.

00:29:44.720 --> 00:29:48.440
They have sell-by dates in their sights.

00:29:52.760 --> 00:30:01.360
'They're putting me through my paces 'before I get to try my first army rations.

00:30:00.400 --> 00:30:05.960
'I'm sure there is an easier way to work up an appetite!'

00:30:05.200 --> 00:30:06.600
Oh, my word!

00:30:06.800 --> 00:30:17.440
'I'm shattered after just a few minutes, but these guys can burn 'up to about 5,000 calories a day, 'so their food has to be just as tough.'

00:30:16.600 --> 00:30:18.840
This is, I think, as far as I can go!

00:30:19.360 --> 00:30:23.520
You got chicken noodles, no?

00:30:22.680 --> 00:30:24.680
No-one wants chicken noodles?

00:30:23.840 --> 00:30:26.960
Anyone want vegetarian?

00:30:26.040 --> 00:30:35.760
'Soldiers in the field are provided with ration packs known as MREs, 'or Meals Ready to Eat.'

00:30:35.120 --> 00:30:37.560
I'm going meatballs in marinara.

00:30:37.960 --> 00:30:41.160
I'm going with the chicken pesto pasta.

00:30:40.240 --> 00:30:47.120
'Each one is 1,300 calories and a perfect nutritional balance.

00:30:46.240 --> 00:30:53.680
'And with a few drops of water to spark a chemical reaction, 'they even heat themselves up.'

00:30:52.720 --> 00:30:57.600
It might not be appealing, eating out of a plastic pack, but you know what?

00:30:56.640 --> 00:31:01.120
When you're absolutely starving, that's what you'll do.

00:31:03.720 --> 00:31:07.880
Now, I've got nice... marinana... marinara meatballs.

00:31:08.240 --> 00:31:18.240
'With over 12 million US ration packs in circulation, 'what is the one food they want to eat more than anything else?'

00:31:17.360 --> 00:31:19.880
Pizza.

00:31:19.080 --> 00:31:20.680
Pizza?

00:31:19.720 --> 00:31:20.760
Pizza would be good.

00:31:19.840 --> 00:31:21.320
And what about the rest of you on pizza?

00:31:20.440 --> 00:31:26.160
Would that be something you would all be happy to eat, a slice of pizza?

00:31:25.280 --> 00:31:27.640
Yeah?

00:31:27.040 --> 00:31:35.080
But creating a military grade pizza is no easy feat.

00:31:34.280 --> 00:31:52.640
All rations have to withstand helicopter drops, being shaken on the back of a truck, and the trickiest thing of all is they have to be able to sit on a shelf in 27 degrees heat for three years and still be edible.

00:31:51.720 --> 00:32:03.480
I've eaten a lot of pizza in my time and I've cooked a fair amount as well, but I've never made one to last three days, never mind three years!

00:32:03.000 --> 00:32:04.400
'And that's what I'm about to do.'

00:32:03.440 --> 00:32:20.400
'Here on the army base, 'military food technologist Michelle Richardson and her team 'need to stop the pizza going off, 'by preventing bacteria from feeding on it.

00:32:19.520 --> 00:32:27.920
'And bacteria love moist environments, 'like pizza sauce or cheese.'

00:32:27.040 --> 00:32:28.960
Are you sick of eating this pizza?

00:32:28.000 --> 00:32:29.680
Um, yes, sometimes.

00:32:30.200 --> 00:32:33.920
I don't really eat pizza as much as I used to!

00:32:33.120 --> 00:32:34.600
I bet you don't!

00:32:35.320 --> 00:32:39.800
'They had to find a way to keep the water and bacteria apart.'

00:32:39.040 --> 00:32:49.960
We use an ingredient called glycerol, and that's, like, one of my favourite ingredients when we are trying to reduce water activity.

00:32:49.120 --> 00:32:50.600
Let's get the glycerol on there, then!

00:32:49.800 --> 00:32:55.120
'The glycerol has been mixed into the pizza sauce.

00:32:54.200 --> 00:33:00.680
'It binds to the water in the sauce, locking it away from the bacteria.'

00:33:01.920 --> 00:33:04.840
'But what about the rest of the ingredients?'

00:33:04.320 --> 00:33:09.720
This looks like pre-grated mozzarella to me, is it?

00:33:08.760 --> 00:33:14.040
It's a 50-50 mix of off-the-shelf mozzarella...

00:33:13.160 --> 00:33:22.960
..Then, there's also what we call a low water activity mozzarella.

00:33:23.600 --> 00:33:29.320
It's made using Osmo technology.

00:33:29.440 --> 00:33:36.880
This is meat that has been dried using pressure and a sweet solution to draw out the water.

00:33:36.000 --> 00:33:37.440
Is that OK?

00:33:36.560 --> 00:33:39.120
That's beautiful.

00:33:38.200 --> 00:33:40.040
You did a better job than I did.

00:33:39.280 --> 00:33:40.600
I'm eating it.

00:33:39.680 --> 00:33:46.920
Hi-tech or not, my pizza still has to be cooked.

00:33:48.640 --> 00:33:52.080
After years in development... but just 20 minutes in the oven, it's ready.

00:33:51.240 --> 00:33:52.400
So, this is my pizza.

00:33:51.640 --> 00:33:54.120
It certainly looks good.

00:33:55.400 --> 00:34:00.640
And it smells pretty good as well.

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:06.800
So, it can last three years and be dropped from a helicopter.

00:34:05.920 --> 00:34:10.480
But will it get the thumbs up from the troops?

00:34:09.600 --> 00:34:12.080
Be honest, what do you think?

00:34:11.239 --> 00:34:14.040
It tastes like pizza.

00:34:13.159 --> 00:34:15.719
That looks fancy.

00:34:14.760 --> 00:34:16.040
Fancy!

00:34:15.320 --> 00:34:17.560
I like that word.

00:34:17.080 --> 00:34:20.920
It's good, it's a little different.

00:34:20.159 --> 00:34:25.360
Out of ten - one being really bad, ten being, obviously, very good - what do you think, Fairfax?

00:34:24.440 --> 00:34:25.639
Eight.

00:34:24.679 --> 00:34:26.199
Eight.

00:34:25.440 --> 00:34:26.760
Eight.

00:34:25.960 --> 00:34:27.199
Eight.

00:34:29.199 --> 00:34:30.600
I'll probably go with seven.

00:34:32.880 --> 00:34:34.080
They're very proud of this pizza, actually.

00:34:33.120 --> 00:34:35.639
I'm going to tell him you all said ten out of ten.

00:34:37.639 --> 00:34:49.960
So, will the three-year pizza make it out of the war zone and onto the high street?

00:34:54.800 --> 00:34:59.360
Chris is seeing how it goes down with the great British public.

00:34:58.400 --> 00:34:59.480
That's amore.

00:35:06.240 --> 00:35:13.520
Now, because the army's long-lasting pizza is so new, none of it has got to the ripe old age of three years yet.

00:35:12.680 --> 00:35:20.880
The ones I have are three months old, which is still some way past the sell-by date of a normal pizza.

00:35:20.320 --> 00:35:21.800
I wonder if I can get anyone to try it.

00:35:21.760 --> 00:35:26.040
Fresh pizza...ish!

00:35:25.160 --> 00:35:26.240
Would you like to try some pizza, sir?

00:35:25.480 --> 00:35:26.800
Are you sure?

00:35:25.960 --> 00:35:29.240
Would you like to try a little bit pizza, sir?

00:35:28.360 --> 00:35:30.200
Are you serious?

00:35:31.400 --> 00:35:33.040
Three months old.

00:35:32.080 --> 00:35:33.840
Imagine that.

00:35:33.560 --> 00:35:38.160
Is it really three months old?

00:35:37.280 --> 00:35:38.440
Yeah.

00:35:37.600 --> 00:35:40.360
It tastes fresh.

00:35:39.440 --> 00:35:43.920
It tastes like cold pizza the next day.

00:35:43.040 --> 00:35:44.680
It tastes three months old.

00:35:43.720 --> 00:35:45.480
Pizza.

00:35:44.880 --> 00:35:50.360
Freshest pizza, only three months old.

00:35:49.440 --> 00:35:51.120
Perfectly safe.

00:35:50.240 --> 00:35:51.680
OK.

00:35:50.800 --> 00:35:52.160
This was better.

00:35:51.200 --> 00:35:52.520
Was it?

00:35:52.760 --> 00:35:56.560
THEY LAUGH That was three minutes old, this is three months old.

00:35:55.800 --> 00:35:57.000
It tastes delicious, actually.

00:35:57.280 --> 00:35:59.760
It tastes really nice.

00:36:00.480 --> 00:36:03.680
So that's a thumbs up, then.

00:36:11.120 --> 00:36:21.200
Over the course of the series, I'll be investigating some of the issues about how we will eat in tomorrow's world.

00:36:20.560 --> 00:36:23.480
Whether we should genetically modify our food.

00:36:22.680 --> 00:36:31.520
How do we tackle the growing crime of food fraud and, firstly, how will change in weather affect what we eat?

00:36:33.680 --> 00:36:48.080
We're often told that the one meal of the day we shouldn't skip is our breakfast and we may take it for granted, but in the future, staples like these could become a rare luxury.

00:36:47.280 --> 00:36:53.280
Because toast is made from bread, which is made from wheat.

00:36:52.320 --> 00:37:06.680
Cereals are made from wheat and wheat, like rice or maize or millet, is a crop that's been bred to deliver huge yields, but finely tuned to today's climate and that climate is changing.

00:37:05.800 --> 00:37:15.640
So, there's a race on to breed crops that can cope with the demands of a hotter climate with more extreme weather.

00:37:14.680 --> 00:37:21.800
And at the forefront of this is a lab in Nottingham run by Professor Malcolm Bennett.

00:37:20.960 --> 00:37:26.440
Malcolm, thank you for letting us into your greenhouse.

00:37:25.520 --> 00:37:26.720
What's going on here?

00:37:25.880 --> 00:37:27.920
What magic are you creating here?

00:37:27.760 --> 00:37:37.320
Well, we're creating the climate of the future, so we're mimicking drought, extreme weather events, all those types of problems we face because of climate change.

00:37:36.480 --> 00:37:41.720
And there are different strains of wheat that you're comparing under different weather conditions?

00:37:42.440 --> 00:37:43.560
Exactly.

00:37:42.720 --> 00:37:47.960
Malcolm is looking for plants that flourish even when it's very dry.

00:37:47.080 --> 00:37:54.720
And to discover how, he's using a CT scanner, like you'd find in a hospital, to look at their roots.

00:37:53.800 --> 00:38:00.920
This one can grow longer roots to reach water deep below the parched surface.

00:38:01.480 --> 00:38:14.360
To future-proof our crops, the plan is to combine the genes of those that can survive a drought with the ones that can provide a lot of wheat.

00:38:13.560 --> 00:38:14.760
But this will take time.

00:38:13.880 --> 00:38:20.480
So, I'm off to America where they're taking more immediate action.

00:38:31.040 --> 00:38:37.000
'The forecast - going to be another warm one.

00:38:36.280 --> 00:38:40.520
'Sunshine and plenty of it 'as we head for a high of 100 this afternoon.'

00:38:39.720 --> 00:38:54.400
The land here in West Texas has long supported a good crop of maize, cotton and wheat, but there's been a drought for the past five years and for some, life has become very difficult.

00:38:55.080 --> 00:39:00.840
I've been farming out here since 1967.

00:39:00.240 --> 00:39:06.840
Steve Williams has a farm just outside the town of El Dorado.

00:39:06.080 --> 00:39:09.960
We're totally dependent on rainfall here.

00:39:09.080 --> 00:39:22.600
I don't have no irrigation and if it doesn't fall out the sky, Mother Nature doesn't send it to us, we don't make a crop.

00:39:22.640 --> 00:39:31.000
But luckily for Steve, here in Texas, it's not just Mother Nature that controls the weather.

00:39:30.080 --> 00:39:31.400
Today, I'm going to witness something quite astonishing.

00:39:30.560 --> 00:39:39.320
Men who take to the sky and make it rain.

00:39:40.920 --> 00:39:42.320
Are you well?

00:39:41.520 --> 00:39:42.680
I'm doing well.

00:39:41.840 --> 00:39:42.960
How about you?

00:39:42.040 --> 00:39:43.600
I'm good.

00:39:43.040 --> 00:39:47.240
I'm looking forward to this, though.

00:39:46.400 --> 00:39:54.320
Let's go see some clouds West Texas style.

00:39:57.560 --> 00:40:03.880
Up ahead of me is another plane piloted by Don Baker.

00:40:03.000 --> 00:40:06.920
So, now we have visual contact with the plane.

00:40:06.040 --> 00:40:07.840
Much higher, is he, than us?

00:40:07.000 --> 00:40:11.760
Yes, he's about 2,000 feet higher than we are.

00:40:11.040 --> 00:40:17.600
Don's plane has nearly 10kg of flares strapped to its wings.

00:40:17.640 --> 00:40:22.440
And he's going to use them to create a rainstorm.

00:40:24.880 --> 00:40:29.720
Clouds have to be ready to rain for this technique to work.

00:40:28.880 --> 00:40:36.000
It's called cloud seeding and it makes it rain sooner and more heavily than it otherwise would have done.

00:40:35.760 --> 00:40:39.120
'Are we aiming for that cloud over there on the right?'

00:40:38.800 --> 00:40:41.160
'That's affirmative.'

00:40:40.200 --> 00:40:44.520
'You can see the flares that are arranged on the wing of the plane.

00:40:43.600 --> 00:40:48.800
'Now, we're going to follow it, fly underneath the cloud.'

00:40:51.240 --> 00:40:56.160
When the cloud seeding plane is in position, under the cloud, Don sets off one of the smoke flares on the wing.

00:40:55.520 --> 00:40:57.960
'Ready for the flares?'

00:40:58.240 --> 00:40:59.720
'That'll be stations four and five.

00:40:59.080 --> 00:41:02.880
Number four and five.'

00:41:05.520 --> 00:41:14.280
Thermals in the cloud suck the smoke up into the centre.

00:41:18.560 --> 00:41:32.840
The smoke contains particles of calcium chloride and silver iodide, which react with tiny droplets of water and ice crystals in the cloud, and grow into bigger droplets.

00:41:31.880 --> 00:41:35.040
As they get heavier, they fall as rain drops.

00:41:35.360 --> 00:41:45.200
The chemicals are used in such small amounts, they're considered harmless and don't contaminate the ground below.

00:41:47.640 --> 00:41:54.120
And this is how you make it rain Texas-style.

00:41:55.200 --> 00:42:07.320
Here in Texas, cloud seeding is so successful, they do it over an area bigger than the whole of Wales.

00:42:06.680 --> 00:42:16.000
And, remarkably, it creates so much extra water, it could supply a major city like San Francisco for a whole year.

00:42:15.160 --> 00:42:16.880
Wow, that is amazing.

00:42:16.280 --> 00:42:24.680
Sitting here, watching this happen, getting a real sense that we really take rain for granted where we come from.

00:42:26.520 --> 00:42:30.080
We don't do any of this stuff, we just stand at a bus stop and we get enough rain.

00:42:29.480 --> 00:42:34.680
Of course, exciting though this is up here, the real benefit is down there.

00:42:34.080 --> 00:42:39.560
Down there, where the people who live, who need this water.

00:42:38.600 --> 00:42:52.120
After cloud seeding, the rain doesn't fall immediately and you can't even guarantee exactly where it will fall, but for farmer Steve Williams, it saved his livelihood.

00:42:51.160 --> 00:43:06.120
When you look up and you see a plane seeding a cloud, and it's raining out of that cloud and you look over there and it's not raining as much out of that cloud, I think most people would become a believer.

00:43:05.280 --> 00:43:12.040
It's helped the crops and it's helped all of the grassland and it's been really good for the area.

00:43:11.720 --> 00:43:15.760
Water is life here.

00:43:28.680 --> 00:43:39.080
We're always on the lookout for new and extraordinary foods of the future and Chris Bavin has one that will challenge our taste buds.

00:43:39.000 --> 00:43:47.120
It's the middle of the night and I'm in New Spitalfields Market in the East End of London.

00:43:46.320 --> 00:43:50.440
The produce in here comes from all over the world.

00:43:49.600 --> 00:43:51.840
Peas, lovely and sweet.

00:43:51.120 --> 00:43:54.120
Yeah, best time of year.

00:43:54.080 --> 00:43:56.480
These traders have seen and sold every fruit and veg going.

00:43:55.840 --> 00:44:00.480
Lovely, lovely bit of Belgian endive.

00:43:59.520 --> 00:44:01.240
Very good!

00:44:02.120 --> 00:44:10.000
But I don't think they've seen one of these - Synsepalum dulcificum, the miracle berry.

00:44:09.120 --> 00:44:12.280
And this fairly innocent-looking piece of fruit does something pretty special.

00:44:15.080 --> 00:44:23.600
It's claimed it can change the way we taste, transforming sour into sweet.

00:44:24.120 --> 00:44:25.360
Right, gentlemen.

00:44:24.480 --> 00:44:26.160
So, with a box of lemons, I'm ready to put it to the test.

00:44:28.840 --> 00:44:29.920
Right.

00:44:29.160 --> 00:44:32.960
So, if you eat that, perfectly natural, don't worry.

00:44:33.840 --> 00:44:35.280
Let it dissolve in your mouth, so don't chew it.

00:44:34.320 --> 00:44:35.400
I just swallowed it.

00:44:36.360 --> 00:44:41.160
The berries grow in West Africa and the ones I've got have been concentrated into pills.

00:44:40.360 --> 00:44:43.440
If you try that now and you try that...

00:44:45.840 --> 00:44:49.440
Sweet.

00:44:48.480 --> 00:44:49.560
Yeah, a totally different fruit.

00:44:48.600 --> 00:44:50.440
It's amazing, isn't it?

00:44:49.640 --> 00:44:54.240
Now it's more like a sugary taste, a very sweet taste in the mouth.

00:44:53.600 --> 00:44:58.480
Like a lemon sherbet, really, like the sweet.

00:44:57.520 --> 00:45:01.960
It's a hell of a difference, yeah.

00:45:01.000 --> 00:45:03.560
Couldn't give one of them pills to my wife, could you?

00:45:02.800 --> 00:45:05.160
Sweeten her up a bit.

00:45:04.240 --> 00:45:06.120
Sweeten her up.

00:45:06.600 --> 00:45:10.880
But is the miracle berry just a party trick?

00:45:10.160 --> 00:45:18.680
Dr Shini Somara is in Japan and they're using it as a weapon in our ongoing battle against sugar addiction.

00:45:17.840 --> 00:45:26.880
In Tokyo, there are now cafes where you can choose to go sugar-free.

00:45:28.720 --> 00:45:35.960
So, I've just had dinner and now it's time for dessert, and this restaurant has a few of my favourites.

00:45:35.120 --> 00:45:42.000
Made without sugar and served with a miracle berry.

00:45:41.240 --> 00:45:43.240
They claim it's a practical sugar alternative.

00:45:43.120 --> 00:45:48.040
First, a bite without the miracle berry.

00:45:49.120 --> 00:45:52.280
That's very tart.

00:45:51.400 --> 00:45:58.160
So then, I eat a berry before another bite.

00:45:57.200 --> 00:45:58.360
Now for the moment of truth.

00:46:00.080 --> 00:46:05.440
That's unbelievably sweet.

00:46:05.120 --> 00:46:20.240
What's actually happening is that a chemical called miraculin in the berry is attaching itself to my taste receptors and that's changing the way they react to the food I'm eating.

00:46:19.440 --> 00:46:26.360
When miraculin meets something acidic, it stimulates the sweet receptors on my tongue.

00:46:25.520 --> 00:46:26.920
Mm!

00:46:25.960 --> 00:46:27.160
Lovely.

00:46:26.240 --> 00:46:30.560
It's amazing, and that was all because of that berry.

00:46:36.160 --> 00:46:44.560
So, eating miracle berries means we could have less sugar and still get that sweet hit.

00:46:43.760 --> 00:46:48.080
But there is one drawback.

00:46:47.600 --> 00:46:53.280
It's an amazing fruit at an amazing price, because it only grows in very specific conditions.

00:46:52.600 --> 00:46:56.560
This little berry can cost anything up to £5.

00:46:55.960 --> 00:47:01.040
But just outside Tokyo, they're trying to make it cheaper.

00:47:01.920 --> 00:47:05.920
This is a dwarf mutant.

00:47:05.120 --> 00:47:20.400
Professor Hiroshi Ezura is using genetic modification to take the sweet gene from the miracle berry and put it in tomato plants.

00:47:19.520 --> 00:47:24.160
So, why did you choose tomatoes as the main medium for this gene?

00:47:23.320 --> 00:47:29.840
Tomato is the number one vegetable produced in Japan and even in the world.

00:47:28.960 --> 00:47:41.240
Tomatoes are cheap and easy to grow, so using them for miraculin instead of miracle berries would drive the cost right down.

00:47:42.120 --> 00:47:49.120
According to the cultivation in our miraculin tomato, the cost would be less than, maybe 200 times less.

00:47:48.520 --> 00:47:51.240
200 times less.

00:47:52.080 --> 00:47:59.440
The plan is to produce miracle tomatoes as fresh fruit and in powdered form.

00:47:58.600 --> 00:48:07.840
But because they're genetically modified, Professor Ezura's tomatoes will be need to be tested and approved before they can leave the lab.

00:48:07.120 --> 00:48:21.000
Right now I can't even try one, but eventually miracle tomatoes could be a small but important step towards curbing our spiralling sugar addiction.

00:48:23.040 --> 00:48:25.640
Tastes.

00:48:24.760 --> 00:48:28.600
We all have our favourites.

00:48:27.720 --> 00:48:40.960
But whether it's the sweetness of strawberries or the sourness of lemons, surely taste is something we'll only ever be able to get from our food and drink.

00:48:40.040 --> 00:48:42.000
Or is it?

00:48:41.280 --> 00:48:50.360
Imagine if, in the future, we didn't need food to create that sensation.

00:48:49.640 --> 00:48:54.920
Imagine if we could do it at the touch of a button.

00:48:54.080 --> 00:49:04.320
At City University in London, a machine is being developed that can trick the brain into thinking it's tasting something that isn't there.

00:49:03.400 --> 00:49:05.760
The project is run by Professor Adrian Cheok.

00:49:04.840 --> 00:49:09.000
Pleasure to meet you.

00:49:08.200 --> 00:49:15.160
His device relies on the way our tongues sense five fundamental flavours.

00:49:14.320 --> 00:49:22.440
We have five different kinds of receptors, which are sour, salty, sweet, bitter and umami taste.

00:49:21.480 --> 00:49:23.000
Would you like to try?

00:49:22.040 --> 00:49:25.880
I'd love to try.

00:49:25.120 --> 00:49:28.120
So, basically, all tastes we know can be reduced down to some combination of these five tastes?

00:49:27.360 --> 00:49:28.960
That's right, yes.

00:49:29.040 --> 00:49:31.760
You can try it for yourself now and tell us, what do you think is the taste of this liquid?

00:49:32.720 --> 00:49:34.600
Are you giving me a particularly strong version, or is it going to be...?

00:49:33.720 --> 00:49:34.880
No, it's mild, I think.

00:49:34.040 --> 00:49:37.000
OK, fine.

00:49:37.760 --> 00:49:38.960
That's sweet.

00:49:38.080 --> 00:49:42.400
That's sickly, but sweet.

00:49:41.480 --> 00:49:48.320
And all children love it, because children are hard-wired to ingest as much energy as possible.

00:49:48.240 --> 00:49:50.320
Oh, is that why kids love sweets?

00:49:49.480 --> 00:49:50.640
OK.

00:49:49.680 --> 00:49:50.880
The second one.

00:49:49.920 --> 00:49:51.240
Hope you like it.

00:49:52.560 --> 00:49:55.160
Hm.

00:49:54.280 --> 00:49:55.480
Urgh!

00:49:54.600 --> 00:49:56.480
That's seawater.

00:49:57.720 --> 00:50:02.040
Yeah, that's a bad day at the beach.

00:50:01.440 --> 00:50:04.400
Then, there's sour.

00:50:04.160 --> 00:50:08.320
And bitter.

00:50:10.640 --> 00:50:12.880
God, they're all horrible tastes!

00:50:12.000 --> 00:50:16.960
And finally umami, the most recent taste to be discovered.

00:50:16.000 --> 00:50:18.320
It just tastes smoky.

00:50:17.400 --> 00:50:18.760
Yes.

00:50:18.360 --> 00:50:21.920
It is often described as a savoury taste.

00:50:21.200 --> 00:50:30.400
Umami is present when you cook meat, pleasurable oily foods, you know, that's why people love to eat barbecues.

00:50:32.040 --> 00:50:39.880
Adrian is trying to recreate those tastes without food, using only electricity to stimulate our taste receptors.

00:50:40.200 --> 00:50:49.720
This device is called an electrical taste machine and what it does, essentially, is it produces an electrical current which will stimulate your taste neurons.

00:50:49.120 --> 00:50:54.960
For example, you can have a virtual sour, salty, sweet taste using this device.

00:50:54.360 --> 00:51:04.600
Simulating taste electronically could lead to all sorts of intriguing possibilities, like sharing it over the internet, so Adrian has high hopes for his device.

00:51:03.840 --> 00:51:13.120
So, you could be watching a TV programme about, you know, MasterChefs and not only see the food, but taste at the same time.

00:51:12.520 --> 00:51:25.160
So, if I have this implement and I am watching MasterChef, they can send me, ping me, essentially, the flavour of the meal that, as they're testing it, I can go, "Mm!"

00:51:24.400 --> 00:51:27.000
I can taste it.

00:51:26.080 --> 00:51:28.440
That's right.

00:51:27.560 --> 00:51:29.720
That's what we hope for in the future.

00:51:28.760 --> 00:51:29.960
You place it on your tongue.

00:51:29.160 --> 00:51:33.400
It's off now, so you won't feel anything at first.

00:51:35.920 --> 00:51:38.320
And now I will turn on the device.

00:51:37.680 --> 00:51:40.440
You should be experiencing a virtual sour taste on your tongue.

00:51:41.480 --> 00:51:48.240
I have to say, I'm not really getting a full burst of sour off that.

00:51:47.400 --> 00:51:50.200
I know, I can feel the extra charge.

00:51:49.240 --> 00:51:51.840
It's like licking a battery, is what it felt like.

00:51:50.960 --> 00:51:52.400
Adrian can change the pattern of electric signals depending on the flavour he wants to create.

00:51:54.480 --> 00:51:55.640
This time we'll try lemon.

00:51:54.800 --> 00:51:56.520
Yeah, OK.

00:52:01.040 --> 00:52:06.760
It's not like you've, literally, opened a lemon on my tongue.

00:52:06.040 --> 00:52:07.240
It's a simulation.

00:52:06.400 --> 00:52:08.400
It's a simulation, yeah.

00:52:08.480 --> 00:52:12.880
It's a really odd sensation, definitely a hint of lemon but also...metallic.

00:52:13.000 --> 00:52:18.280
Do you have any tequila?

00:52:18.800 --> 00:52:22.320
ADRIAN LAUGHS I'm thinking, you know, while I've got the taste in my mouth, I'm just saying.

00:52:21.400 --> 00:52:22.840
When life gives you lemons...

00:52:25.240 --> 00:52:35.120
Adrian's device is still some way off from making it into our homes, but the idea of digitising taste is very intriguing.

00:52:34.760 --> 00:52:44.760
The thought of tricking your brain or being able to e-mail a taste, that's all incredible and he may be on the path to that, but right now, he's a very nice man who puts a very bad taste in your mouth.

00:52:43.960 --> 00:52:45.400
Argh!

00:52:44.520 --> 00:52:46.000
Argh!

00:52:45.200 --> 00:52:49.440
I need a chocolate bar.

00:52:56.840 --> 00:53:04.640
Earlier, we asked a group of truckers to eat seaweed pills with every meal.

00:53:03.800 --> 00:53:15.080
Brand-new science suggests the seaweed would stop them absorbing some of the fat they ate.

00:53:14.200 --> 00:53:17.920
So, have they lost any weight?

00:53:17.160 --> 00:53:23.600
After four weeks, we've brought our truckers back to the caff to see if the seaweed alginate has worked.

00:53:24.680 --> 00:53:25.760
Good morning, guys.

00:53:24.920 --> 00:53:26.160
How did we all find it?

00:53:25.360 --> 00:53:26.640
How did you get on?

00:53:25.840 --> 00:53:28.040
Enjoyed it?

00:53:27.200 --> 00:53:28.600
Yeah?

00:53:27.720 --> 00:53:31.040
Apart from the tablets.

00:53:30.160 --> 00:53:32.200
You enjoyed it apart from the actual thing you had to do!

00:53:32.800 --> 00:53:37.920
The trial is being run by Dr Matt Wilcox.

00:53:37.160 --> 00:53:45.120
He's going to compare every trucker's weight now with a month ago.

00:53:46.640 --> 00:53:54.240
So, will the seaweed alginate have helped the fat they've eaten to pass straight through them rather than end up on their waistline?

00:53:54.320 --> 00:53:57.680
I lost 1kg.

00:53:56.840 --> 00:53:58.280
1kg.

00:53:57.360 --> 00:53:58.800
Yeah.

00:53:57.920 --> 00:53:59.520
Very good.

00:53:59.080 --> 00:54:03.040
And didn't change diet at all.

00:54:02.240 --> 00:54:03.440
And what about you?

00:54:02.480 --> 00:54:04.000
I lost 1.3kg.

00:54:03.080 --> 00:54:04.640
Lost 2kg.

00:54:03.720 --> 00:54:05.920
Lost 2kg?

00:54:06.560 --> 00:54:07.960
Yeah.

00:54:07.280 --> 00:54:10.120
Without even trying.

00:54:09.240 --> 00:54:10.280
And yourself?

00:54:09.440 --> 00:54:18.200
About the same but I've, honestly, eaten some of the worst dross you could ever imagine in the last five weeks.

00:54:17.400 --> 00:54:18.640
On purpose.

00:54:17.760 --> 00:54:19.200
Yeah.

00:54:18.240 --> 00:54:21.440
I've had a guilt-free month.

00:54:20.480 --> 00:54:23.720
Amazing results for the truckers.

00:54:23.760 --> 00:54:24.840
So what about me?

00:54:25.240 --> 00:54:28.480
Oh, hello, you all want to see mine?

00:54:27.560 --> 00:54:28.920
That's nice, innit?

00:54:28.000 --> 00:54:29.600
So it looks like you've put on weight.

00:54:30.600 --> 00:54:32.120
Put on weight!

00:54:31.960 --> 00:54:34.960
Sweet.

00:54:34.280 --> 00:54:36.480
The only person to put on weight was me.

00:54:35.600 --> 00:54:37.680
That's fairly embarrassing.

00:54:36.960 --> 00:54:39.920
Thank you very much.

00:54:40.840 --> 00:54:49.480
Now I have to fess up, I forgot to take some of my pills, which is probably why I didn't lose weight, but the others have done brilliantly.

00:54:48.720 --> 00:55:02.360
On average, they lost 1.5kg in just four weeks and absorbed 6% less fat.

00:55:01.640 --> 00:55:12.200
Now, that might not sound like much, but over a year, that's 4kg of fat each that wouldn't have hit their waistlines.

00:55:12.560 --> 00:55:16.440
Or a whopping 48 fry-ups.

00:55:15.720 --> 00:55:18.560
Look at this.

00:55:19.640 --> 00:55:26.560
I mean, just the heat coming off of 48 cooked breakfast, I'm sweating!

00:55:25.760 --> 00:55:40.120
Seaweed alginate tablets aren't available in the shops yet as there are more trials to be done, but it's not difficult to imagine them on sale in every caff in Britain.

00:55:39.400 --> 00:55:48.360
I must admit, I was sceptical about this trial, but those results seem to show that seaweed could play a big part in all our future diets.

00:55:47.520 --> 00:55:51.720
Imagine in a few years, places like this full of skinny truckers.

00:55:54.960 --> 00:55:57.400
So, to recap.

00:55:56.560 --> 00:56:06.320
In the future, our children's lunchboxes will be filled with everlasting food, robots will do our farming and sweet treats will be guilt-free.

00:56:05.480 --> 00:56:08.320
But there's more.

00:56:07.480 --> 00:56:15.400
Next week - Angela comes face-to-face with the chef of the future.

00:56:14.600 --> 00:56:21.840
In Milan, Shini checks out the supermarket of tomorrow.

00:56:23.280 --> 00:56:29.560
Chris learns how to grow fruit and veg in one of the most hostile locations on the planet.

00:56:28.680 --> 00:56:38.400
And I reveal how Britain's online supermarkets bring us futuristic shopping today.

00:56:37.440 --> 00:56:39.080
Before we go, I want to introduce you to an incredible new plant.

00:56:40.160 --> 00:56:46.520
A plant so amazing, it has its own greenhouse.

00:56:45.760 --> 00:56:47.240
Not that one, this one.

00:56:46.600 --> 00:56:47.840
This is its greenhouse.

00:56:46.880 --> 00:56:52.640
It's called the TomTato.

00:56:51.760 --> 00:57:07.160
And the reason it's called that is because, well, this is one plant where above ground you can grow tomatoes off it, just like normal, but underground, off the same plant, potatoes are growing.

00:57:06.240 --> 00:57:08.200
There is no miraculous science at work here.

00:57:07.480 --> 00:57:14.440
These two plants have just been spliced together when they were young.

00:57:13.480 --> 00:57:15.600
It's possible because potatoes and tomatoes are related.

00:57:14.840 --> 00:57:28.320
And I can tell you that this is an amazing thing, because it allows us to marshal the limited resources we have, or use smaller bits of land, but it's amazing because this is a plant that will give you chips and ketchup in one.

00:57:27.360 --> 00:57:28.840
Truly, we live in an age of wonders.

00:57:28.000 --> 00:57:29.120
See you next week.

00:57:28.280 --> 00:57:29.520
You'd better come in with a brilliant product.

00:57:28.560 --> 00:57:29.960
Don't try and mislead us.

00:57:29.000 --> 00:57:30.280
Have I come across that badly?

00:57:29.440 --> 00:57:30.840
Keep your cool.

00:57:47.160 --> 00:57:48.240
Well, but, erm...

00:58:06.560 --> 00:58:07.640
Get it right and we might help your business reach the next level.

00:58:15.840 --> 00:58:17.120
But there's no promises.

00:58:16.160 --> 00:58:17.880
A new BBC Two comedy panel show all about people.

00:58:19.280 --> 00:58:20.360
Each week, everyone we talk about will share the same names.

00:58:19.440 --> 00:58:20.840
Promise it's very simple - not at all confusing.

00:58:20.080 --> 00:58:21.360
So join me, Sue Perkins... ..me, Sue Widdicombe... ..and me, Sue Osman.

00:58:20.400 --> 00:58:21.520
That's Insert Name Here...
